<details class="gdetails" id="gpl-p8s">
<summary>Dr. Patel: Sun exposure slide by slide, what the lecture adds (56 slides)</summary>
<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-1">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 1&ndash;5</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>Her objectives are where her questions come from</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 1, 3, 4, 5</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Learning objectives 1 to 5 (Patel Sun Exposure slide 3)</h5>
<p>Objectives 1 to 5 are Objectives 1 to 5 above (interview, condition, self-care candidate, goals, non-drug therapy). Exam cue: her questions come from the objectives (T@46619 11/12).</p>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Learning objectives 6 and 7 (Patel Sun Exposure slide 4)</h5>
<p>Objective 6 asks for dosing frequency and maximum days of use for the ingredients highlighted in lecture, onset, length of treatment, adverse effects, contraindications, interactions, and follow-up. The numbers in this lecture that fit it are the 40 to 80 minute reapplication interval, the 1 to 2 day limit for systemic analgesics in sunburn, the 7-day follow-up, and the 3-month limit for hydroquinone.</p>
<p>Exam cue: when she gives a hint, that is what she wants the class to know.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;this is what I want you to know. Make note of that.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@1183</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-2">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 6&ndash;8</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>Which UV rays reach which layer of skin, and the skin-tone scale that guides each recommendation</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 6, 7, 8</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>UV light has two types of radiation reaching the skin (Patel Sun Exposure slide 6)</h5>
<p>The picture shows UV-A and UV-B entering the skin layers. UVA reaches deeper, the dermis, and UVB stays in the epidermis. She sent the class to Dr. Smith and Dr. Sikazwe for the difference between the two, and she uses the terms UVA and UVB in her own slides.</p>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>UV radiation: all UV is potentially hazardous (Patel Sun Exposure slide 7)</h5>
<p>The slide lists UVC, UVB and UVA with wavelengths in nanometers. For recommendations, the two points she stressed are that the atmosphere absorbs much of the UVB and most sunscreens block it, while UVA is the largest part of sunlight, goes deeper, and is blocked by broad-spectrum sunscreens. That is why broad spectrum, not SPF alone, is the key term in the later slides.</p>
<p>The slide also says UVB is the region most responsible for sunburn and produces more DNA lesions.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;It is blocked by broad spectrum sunscreens. That is the biggest key term there.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@48262</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Fitzpatrick scale of skin tone (Patel Sun Exposure slide 8)</h5>
<p>The scale runs from I, very fair, always burns and cannot tan, to VI, dark brown, never burns and always tans. She uses it to tailor advice, together with the patient's genetics, and she names Fitzpatrick I and II as the patients with the worst outcomes from UV radiation (T@48974 11/12). Type IV is olive, rarely burns and always tans, and the patient case on slide 52 is a type IV patient.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-3">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 9&ndash;11</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>Who is at highest risk from UV radiation, and which drugs add to that risk</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 9, 10, 11</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Patients at highest risk of UV-induced problems (Patel Sun Exposure slide 9)</h5>
<p>Highest risk: Fitzpatrick I and II, light hair, freckles, a history of blistering sunburns, a prior UV-caused growth, a UV-induced skin disorder such as skin cancer or DNA repair deficiency, a family member with melanoma, tanning beds, photosensitizing or immunosuppressive drugs, and autoimmune disease. The lecture ties this to drugs the class has already seen: tetracyclines are the drug class, and doxycycline is the most common photosensitizing antibiotic. A patient taking doxycycline falls under the photosensitizing-drug item on the slide.</p>
<p>She described tanning beds as extra ray exposure beyond sunlight.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;that is the most common photosensitizing antibiotic&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@50043</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Medications associated with increased sun sensitivity (Patel Sun Exposure slide 10)</h5>
<p>The slide covers antibiotics, antifungals, antihistamines, statins, diuretics, NSAIDs, combined oral contraceptives and estrogens, phenothiazines, psoralens, retinoids, sulfonamides, sulfonylureas for type 2 diabetes, and alpha-hydroxy acids in cosmetics. Her instruction is to mark the ones used for the skin conditions in her course (list in Objective 1, block 2), so the student can educate the patient.</p>
<p>The antibiotic names on the slide include ciprofloxacin, doxycycline, levofloxacin, ofloxacin, tetracycline and trimethoprim.</p>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Medications that may contribute to photosensitivity (Patel Sun Exposure slide 11)</h5>
<p>Slide 10 holds drugs associated with increased sun sensitivity. Slide 11 holds drug groups that may contribute: anticonvulsants, antidepressants and antipsychotics, antihypertensives and diuretics, NSAIDs, oral contraceptives, and retinoids. She said the second list is possible, not definite, and that a few drugs appear on both; by the two slides these are diuretics, NSAIDs, oral contraceptives and retinoids.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;it's not definite in terms of if they can or cannot&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@50764</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-4">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 12&ndash;15</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>Who should use sunscreen, how to choose it, and the SPF number she keys for the exam</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 12, 13, 14, 15</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sun safety measures (Patel Sun Exposure slide 12)</h5>
<p>The measures are avoiding the high-risk window, checking the UV index, sunscreen, sunglasses, a hat, clothing, shade, and no tanning devices. The rays are strongest between 10 a.m. and 4 p.m., and she tells pharmacists to point patients to the UV index in a phone weather app. The slide adds that a cloudy or overcast day is not protective, a hat brim of 4 inches, and tight-weave or high-SPF clothing.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;within our weather apps, you can educate our patients to check the UV index&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@51217</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Who should use sunscreen and who is excluded (Patel Sun Exposure slide 13)</h5>
<p>Everyone should wear sunscreen, because UV radiation causes premature aging and skin cancer. She stressed that patients who think they do not burn or tan still need it, so Fitzpatrick type does not remove the recommendation. The exclusions are age under 6 months and allergy to all sunscreen ingredients; for an infant she recommends clothing, hats and shade instead. The slide adds that skin cancers raise the risk of other cancers.</p>
<div class="g2-note"><span class="g2-k">Both versions</span><p>Slide: allergy to all sunscreen ingredients. Lecture: allergic to any of the ingredients (T@52014). Key: all ingredients (slide).</p></div>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;everybody, even anybody that thinks that they're not gonna burn or tan&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@51459</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunscreen selection: four questions (Patel Sun Exposure slide 14)</h5>
<p>The slide gives four steps: compare spectrum coverage, choose among active and inert ingredients, match to the patient's history and Fitzpatrick score, and match to the planned activity. She called broad spectrum the main point to pay attention to. Activity changes the product: for swimming or heavy sweating she recommends a sunscreen for that activity. Fragrance-free and hypoallergenic products are other choices. She said "waterproof"; slides 36 and 55 and her case answer use the label term water resistant.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;The biggest thing to pay attention to is broad spectrum&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@52237</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunscreen selection continued: SPF (Patel Sun Exposure slide 15)</h5>
<p>SPF indicates only UVB protection, which is why the product must also be broad spectrum to cover UVA. The slide's ladder is SPF 15 blocks 93% of UVB, SPF 30 blocks 97%, SPF 50 blocks 98%, and SPF 100 blocks 99%. For exam purposes the number is SPF 15 or higher with broad spectrum; the slide prefers SPF 30+, and for a patient she said the higher the SPF the better.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;SPF is only indicated to cover UVB.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@52976</span></p>
<p>&ldquo;Education purposes, patient-specific, personal purposes, the higher the SPF the better&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@53533</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-5">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 16&ndash;18</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>Physical and chemical sunscreen ingredients and which ones she wants named as most effective</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 16, 17, 18</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunscreen ingredients: physical and chemical (Patel Sun Exposure slide 16)</h5>
<p>Physical sunscreens, zinc oxide and titanium dioxide, are opaque and reflect and scatter UV rays. Chemical sunscreens absorb the rays and then block them. Because chemical ingredients absorb the radiation, they can irritate the skin and even cause burning, which is the reverse of what the patient wants; physical ingredients reflect the rays, and slide 17 points sensitive skin to the physical pair. The slide lists many chemical ingredients, including padimate O, meradimate, oxybenzone, avobenzone, octinoxate and ecamsule, and shows an overlap for combination products.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Chemical ingredients also tend to irritate the skin&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@53859</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunscreen ingredient choice for sensitive skin and photosensitive patients (Patel Sun Exposure slide 17)</h5>
<ul>
<li><b>Sensitive skin or earlier allergic reactions:</b> avoid ortho-aminobenzoic acid (PABA); consider a GRASE sunscreen, zinc oxide or titanium dioxide</li>
<li><b>Sun-induced disorder or photosensitizing drugs:</b> reduce sun exposure; a broad-spectrum product such as avobenzone with padimate O or octocrylene, meradimate, titanium dioxide or benzophenones</li>
</ul>
<p>She added that few products on the market are free of chemical ingredients, and that a product combining a chemical ingredient with titanium dioxide is a mixture of chemical and physical (T@54711&ndash;54945 11/12). For patient advice the physical pair is most preferred.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Physical ingredients, zinc oxide, titanium dioxide is most preferred.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@55941</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Proposed GRASE status of sunscreen ingredients (Patel Sun Exposure slide 18)</h5>
<p>The FDA table puts zinc oxide and titanium dioxide in the GRASE (generally recognized as safe and effective) column, aminobenzoic acid (PABA) and trolamine salicylate in the not-GRASE column for safety concerns, and cinoxate, dioxybenzone, ensulizole, homosalate, meradimate, octinoxate, octisalate, octocrylene, padimate O, sulisobenzone, oxybenzone and avobenzone in the column that needs more data. Her emphasis was a single point: know the two ingredients that are GRASE and most effective.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;I want you to understand what two ingredients are most effective&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@55141</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-6">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 19&ndash;23</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>The sun-protection interview, why chemical absorption matters, how much and how often to apply, and vitamin D</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Medical history questions for sun protection (Patel Sun Exposure slide 19)</h5>
<p>The slide asks about allergies, skin sensitivities, current medications and CAM, exclusion from sunscreen, photosensitizing drugs, earlier reactions, and exposure pattern. She explained that the answers decide the recommendation, and that when nothing excludes the patient the default is a broad-spectrum product of SPF 15 or higher.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;We're gonna recommend a sunscreen that's broad-spectrum, greater than or equal to SPF 15.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@55824</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunscreen ingredient absorption into the blood (Patel Sun Exposure slide 20)</h5>
<p>The chart shows the average maximum blood level of avobenzone, homosalate, octisalate, octocrylene, octinoxate and oxybenzone from FDA testing. The red line is the FDA threshold of 0.5 ng/mL on the slide, which she said means chemical ingredients could cause harm. Every bar on the chart is above the line, and oxybenzone is by far the highest. Some products still use that ingredient and some have dropped it, so the pharmacist's job is to read the ingredient list and recommend accordingly.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;it is our job as pharmacists to educate and provide the best recommendation&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@57110</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>How to apply sunscreen (Patel Sun Exposure slide 21)</h5>
<p>Adults apply about 1 ounce to all exposed skin, 15 minutes before UV exposure, and reapply after moisture or towel drying and every 40 to 80 minutes. The biggest causes of sunburn are not enough product, not often enough, and not evenly. When a patient says sunscreen failed, the first check is how it was applied.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;they'll say I use sunscreen, but I still burnt&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@57379</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunscreen application study: uncovered skin (Patel Sun Exposure slide 22)</h5>
<p>The images from a coverage study use red to mark skin the sunscreen did not cover. Her reading is that red areas are where the patient did not apply evenly, and the back and the feet were the most common misses. The slide's result: females 13.57% uncovered against males 21.94% (P=0.0045). The counseling use is to apply evenly, often and regularly.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;the feet tend to be the most common&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@58161</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Vitamin D while wearing sunscreen (Patel Sun Exposure slide 23)</h5>
<p>Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen with high UVA protection does not compromise vitamin D status in healthy people. Patients often say they skip sunscreen or going outside because of vitamin D, and this is the answer to them. The slide adds that vitamin D screening and supplementation are recommended in photosensitivity disorders and other high-risk groups (elderly, Fitzpatrick IV to VI).</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;So in general, it will not compromise vitamin D levels.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@58738</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-7">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 24&ndash;28</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>The timeline of sunburn, the scale of skin cancer, the lasting skin and eye changes, and the ABCDE rule</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 24, 25, 26, 27, 28</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunburn: what happens and when (Patel Sun Exposure slide 24)</h5>
<p>Symptoms start about 4 hours after exposure, erythema peaks at around 24 hours, and the burn may last up to 5 days. A patient therefore feels fine outdoors and notices the burn after coming inside, which explains why a patient who had no pain during the activity can still be burned. The slide also names peripheral vasodilation in the epidermis and an inflammatory reaction with lymphocyte infiltrates.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;they come inside and then they notice that they're burnt&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@59006</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Skin cancer statistics and the base tan message (Patel Sun Exposure slide 25)</h5>
<p>The CDC graphic says nearly 5 million people are treated for skin cancers each year at a cost of more than $8 billion, and about 76,000 new melanoma cases and 9,000 deaths; it also says most skin cancers can be prevented, and a poster reads a base tan is not a safe tan. She said "over 5 million" (T@59450).</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;And this can all be prevented.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@59585</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Skin and eye changes from sun exposure (Patel Sun Exposure slide 26)</h5>
<p>The slide shows cataract, pterygium, age spots, wrinkles and melasma as the skin and eye changes from sun exposure. She tied them to protection: sunglasses protect the eyes, a hat protects the face, and shade and sunscreen protect the skin.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;not wearing sunglasses, not protecting your face with a hat&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@59855</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Long-term risks of UV: three skin cancers and premature aging (Patel Sun Exposure slide 27)</h5>
<p>The slide lists squamous cell carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma, both common on sun-exposed face, head and neck (basal cell also on the trunk), and melanoma, which can occur anywhere and is more common on skin with intermittent intense exposure, torso and legs. The lecture gives the appearance that separates cancer from a mole: an irregular border as the sign of cancer rather than a mole, squamous cell lighter in color, basal cell a little larger and a little darker pink, and melanoma the darkest. Premature aging means wrinkling and hyperpigmentation.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;that irregularity of the border is there&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@60223</span></p>
<p>&ldquo;Melanoma, worst case scenario is the darkest and often found on the body.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@60354</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>ABCDE rule for skin cancer (Patel Sun Exposure slide 28)</h5>
<p>The rule is asymmetry, irregular border, uneven color, diameter, and evolving. Diameter on the slide means a change in size, usually an increase. Color can be black, brown and tan; the slide adds gray, red, pink, blue and white areas, while she named white, pink and red. Evolving means the mole changed over weeks or months; she described one mole progressing to the next.</p>
<div class="g2-note"><span class="g2-k">Both versions</span><p>Slide: 'most are larger than 6 millimeters wide (about 1/4 inch wide)'. Lecture: 'about 6 millimeters', 'like the largest' (T@60842). Key: larger than 6 mm, about 1/4 inch (slide).</p></div>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;A B C D E rule applies here again&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@60584</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-8">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 29&ndash;32</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>How sunburn is treated: goals, who is not a self-care patient, recommended products, and aloe</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 29, 30, 31, 32</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunburn: treatment goals and plan (Patel Sun Exposure slide 29)</h5>
<p>Goals are hydration, pain relief, physical protection, lower chance of infection or scarring, and few drug adverse effects. Initial treatment is non-drug: if the burn happened in the past 20 minutes, immerse in cool water or use cool wet cloths. After that, any combination of indicated OTC products is acceptable, and the choice depends on patient preference, allergies and what has worked before. She tied the infection and scarring goal to peeling, oozing and blistering skin.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;what they may be allergic to, what has worked in the past&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@61542</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunburn: exclusions for self-treatment (Patel Sun Exposure slide 30)</h5>
<p>The exclusions are in Objective 3, block 1; the slide's signs of infection are red areas or streaking moving away from open blisters and pus draining from open blisters.</p>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunburn treatment: recommended products (Patel Sun Exposure slide 31)</h5>
<p>Systemic analgesics are listed first: an anti-inflammatory drug, an NSAID or acetaminophen, to reduce pain, erythema and edema, used for the first 1 to 2 days. Skin protectants, such as cocoa butter or petrolatum, help rehydration and irritation, are applied as often as needed, and are started only after 24 hours, because the skin is very sensitive early. She told the class to avoid products with alcohol or witch hazel. Her examples: Tylenol or ibuprofen (lecture, T@62020 11/12). The slide adds that systemic analgesics last longer than topical anesthetics.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;any products that have alcohol in them, witch hazel&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@62310</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunburn: aloe vera as alternative treatment (Patel Sun Exposure slide 32)</h5>
<p>The slide says aloe vera is used topically on intact skin, may lower pain by inhibiting bradykinin and the inflammatory process, is not FDA approved for burns, and should not be taken internally because of drug interactions and adverse effects. The lecture adds the counseling point for products: read the ingredient list, because a bottle that lists aloe juice mixed with glycerin and other ingredients contains only about 5% aloe (lecture). She said the lack of FDA approval is partly because aloe is a natural ingredient, like supplements and vitamins, and that it has still shown data to be very effective.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;make sure that it says pure aloe vera juice or pure aloe vera gel&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@64152</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-9">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 33&ndash;35</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>Products not to recommend for sunburn, follow-up timing, and the case answers she gave</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 33, 34, 35</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunburn treatment: not recommended (Patel Sun Exposure slide 33)</h5>
<p>First aid antiseptics and topical anesthetics are not recommended for routine use. She said antiseptics can irritate and burn the skin even more. The slide's limit if they are used anyway is small areas (1 to 2% body surface area) of intact skin, no more than 3 to 4 times a day, with a risk of hypersensitivity and systemic symptoms.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;first aid antiseptics cause it can irritate and burn the skin even more&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@64451</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sunburn follow-up (Patel Sun Exposure slide 34)</h5>
<p>Refer to a physician if the burn has progressed or worsened after 24 to 48 hours, or has not healed in 7 days. She stated that the 7-day policy used earlier in the course applies here as well.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;If it doesn't heal within 7 days, 7-day policy applies here as well&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@64740</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Practice case: sun exposure in a 21-year-old woman (Patel Sun Exposure slide 35)</h5>
<p>The case is a Fitzpatrick 2 woman with a one-day painful sunburn after a weekend with no sunscreen, no vesicles or peeling, cold showers helping, taking Yazmin. Her three poll answers (options on her PollEv page, PollEv 11/12):</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Pain and tenderness:</b> A, oral ibuprofen (the systemic analgesic of slide 31)</li>
<li><b>Daily skin cancer prevention:</b> D, a water-resistant broad-spectrum sunscreen, SPF 50; the other options had an inappropriate SPF or lacked broad spectrum or water resistance</li>
<li><b>Her 4-month-old:</b> A, protective clothing and restricted sun exposure (no sunscreen under 6 months)</li>
</ul>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Remember, 6 months of age, OK, so not 7, not 8.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@66259</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-10">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 36&ndash;37</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>The sun exposure counseling points she flagged as hints</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 36, 37</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sun exposure counseling points for patients (Patel Sun Exposure slide 36)</h5>
<ul>
<li><b>Which:</b> broad-spectrum SPF 15 or higher (flagged); the most substantive sunscreen available</li>
<li><b>How much:</b> 1 ounce over exposed areas (flagged); avoid eyes and mucous membranes</li>
<li><b>When and how often:</b> 15 minutes before exposure; reapply at least every 40 to 80 minutes (flagged), more often with swimming or sweating or a product that is not water resistant</li>
<li>Limit sun from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; check expiration dates</li>
</ul>
<p> Expired product is discarded because it is no longer effective, and water activity calls for a water-resistant broad-spectrum product.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Know how much to apply. Know how often to apply.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@66812</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Sun exposure counseling points continued (Patel Sun Exposure slide 37)</h5>
<p>The slide covers hats, sunglasses and clothing; snow and sand reflecting UV; daily sunscreen and clothing with photosensitizing drugs; stopping a product that causes redness, itching, rash or exaggerated sunburn; and referring a suspicious lesion. She added that a cold winter day does not remove the need, though less is needed than in summer, because snow still reflects sunlight. For a lesion the goal is early detection, since it may need only removal. In response to a student's comment on face sunscreens, she recommended a face product with SPF 15 or higher.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;If the product causes redness, itching, rash, you're stopping the product.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@68005</span></p>
<p>&ldquo;catch it early. That is the biggest key.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@68231</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-11">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 38&ndash;42</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>What photoaging is, how it differs from chronologic aging, and how it is prevented</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 38, 39, 40, 41, 42</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Photoaging and its classification by age (Patel Sun Exposure slide 39)</h5>
<p>The classification grades photoaging as mild, moderate, advanced or severe by the patient's age and by the wrinkles. Type I is mild, no wrinkles, 20 to 30 years of age; type II is moderate, wrinkles in motion, 30 to 40; type III is advanced, wrinkles at rest, 50 or older; type IV is severe, only wrinkles with a yellow-gray color, 60 or older. The table is a Glogau classification on the slide. The slide adds that fair-skinned North Americans show photodamage by age 50.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;depends on the age of the patient as well as how severe the wrinkles&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@68874</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Chronologic aging compared with photoaging (Patel Sun Exposure slide 40)</h5>
<p>Chronologic aging brings thin skin, fine wrinkles, xerosis, laxity, seborrheic keratoses and senile angiomas. Photoaging brings atrophic skin, coarse wrinkles, leathery skin, lentigines, a bronzed or yellow cobblestone appearance, telangiectasis, bruising and sun-induced dermatitis. Her relationship is the exam-relevant one: aging happens regardless, and sun exposure makes the same processes worse, adding hyperpigmentation and sunspots. She named collagen loss and loss of elasticity for ordinary aging.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;photoaging advances the process of aging of the skin&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@69627</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Skin anti-aging approaches (Patel Sun Exposure slide 41)</h5>
<p>The slide groups approaches as cosmetic care, topical medications, invasive procedures, systemic agents and prevention. She called it a reference slide of options for the patient, said cosmetic care is the most common option, and returned to prevention, including a sunscreen on the face in any weather or season.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Wear that sunscreen on your face, no matter what weather it is&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@70517</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Photoaging prevention (Patel Sun Exposure slide 42)</h5>
<p>Reduce UV exposure (sunscreen, protective clothing, sunglasses and hats) and other environmental risks (air pollution, smoking); see Objective 4, block 3.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-12">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 43&ndash;46</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>The photoaging drug classes she named, how retinoids and hydroxy acids are used, and why peels are not an OTC option</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 43, 44, 45, 46</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Photoaging: pharmacologic treatments (Patel Sun Exposure slide 43)</h5>
<ul>
<li><b>Topical retinoids:</b> OTC retinol and retinyl palmitate (the skin must convert them to retinoic acid, which limits efficacy); Rx tretinoin and tazarotene (can reduce wrinkles and repair collagen over 6 to 12 months)</li>
<li><b>Keratolytics</b> (AHAs, BHAs, polyhydroxy acids): may irritate; start every other night for 1 week, increase as tolerated to at most twice daily</li>
<li><b>Antioxidant vitamins:</b> E, C, coenzyme Q10</li>
<li><b>Depigmenting agents:</b> hydroquinone, monobenzone</li>
</ul>
<p>She went through them by purpose: retinoids, keratolytics, vitamins E, C and coenzyme Q10, and hydroquinone as the most commonly used brightening agent. She said the ingredients in products marketed for brightening or aging are most likely among these.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;brightening of skin is hydroquinone, and that is most commonly used&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@70896</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Vitamin A derivatives: retinoids (Patel Sun Exposure slide 44)</h5>
<p>Retinoids increase keratinocyte cell division, build collagen and elastic fibers, reduce melanin production, and improve wrinkles, mottled pigment and texture. Benefit requires long-term daily use and may take months. They are the same drugs used for acne, so many anti-aging products contain them. The side effects are dryness, scaling and phototoxicity, so a moisturizer is mixed in and the sunscreen goes on after the skincare routine. The slide's way to reduce them is a cream-based moisturizer with SPF 30+ in the daytime and the retinoid product at night or on alternate days.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;we're always applying that sunscreen after the skincare routine is completed&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@71941</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Keratolytics: alpha hydroxy acids (Patel Sun Exposure slide 45)</h5>
<p>The slide names lactic, glycolic, citric and malic acids, which increase dermal thickness, improve collagen and elastic fibers, reduce hyperpigmentation and increase exfoliation. In lecture she tied them back to the acne lecture and stated two effects: less hyperpigmentation and more collagen. The slide 10 list also includes alpha-hydroxy acids in cosmetics as a sun-sensitizing ingredient.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;AHAs play a role too, so we talked about this in acne.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@72023</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Keratolytic chemical peels (Patel Sun Exposure slide 46)</h5>
<p>The table classifies peels by depth: superficial, medium and deep, with the agents, depth of penetration and side effects. She said peels are usually done by medical spas and aestheticians, and she would not recommend an over-the-counter peel for use on one's own skin because of the side effects and risks. Deep peels are professional medical use only. The table lists deep-peel risks including cardiotoxicity and arrhythmia, hepatotoxicity and nephrotoxicity.</p>
<p>Her examples by depth: superficial AHA and BHA, medium salicylic acid, glycolic acid and TCA, deep TCA with other ingredients.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;that is only professional medical use&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@72649</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-13">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 47&ndash;51</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>Hydroquinone limits, the FDA position, antioxidants, product labels and plant extracts</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 47, 48, 49, 50, 51</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Depigmentation: hydroquinone (Patel Sun Exposure slide 47)</h5>
<p>Hydroquinone reduces sun-associated hyperpigmentation, melasma and lentigines. Use is limited to 3 months and sunscreen is worn after it. The slide lists combination products with hydroquinone 4% (slide-only figure). She added that it is not FDA approved for OTC sale in the US, yet products with it are still sold and are not FDA regulated, so the pharmacist educates on strength and safe use; a patient who chooses it starts low and increases slowly, and licensed aestheticians are best placed to apply it.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;it is not FDA approved for OTC sale in the US&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@73083</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>FDA skin product safety: hydroquinone (Patel Sun Exposure slide 48)</h5>
<p>The FDA sheet says skin lightening products with hydroquinone are not FDA approved for OTC sale, are used to correct uneven tone and remove dark spots, can cause permanent discoloration (ochronosis), rashes and facial swelling, and should be used only when prescribed by a licensed health care provider.</p>
<div class="g2-note"><span class="g2-k">Both versions</span><p>Slide 48 (FDA): not approved for OTC sale; use only when prescribed by a licensed provider. Lecture: products are still sold OTC, and the pharmacist educates a patient who uses one: 3 months, sunscreen, start low (T@73173). Key: the FDA statements.</p></div>
<div class="g2-note"><span class="g2-k">General source, not from the course</span><p>FDA (April 2022): OTC hydroquinone products have not been legally marketable since September 23, 2020; only prescription Tri-Luma is FDA-approved. https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-communications/fda-works-protect-consumers-potentially-harmful-otc-skin-lightening-products</p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Topical antioxidants (Patel Sun Exposure slide 49)</h5>
<p>The slide lists niacinamide (vitamin B3), ascorbic acid (vitamin C), D-alpha-tocopherol (vitamin E), coenzyme Q-10 and alpha-lipoic acid, with some evidence of protection but no proven formula for delivery to the skin. She named niacinamide and vitamin C as the most common in skincare products and described vitamin C as brightening. Asked how long to use a product before switching, she answered as for acne: consistent daily use for at least a month, in line with the at-least-four-weeks point on slide 53.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;at least more than a month to really see some efficacy&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@74891</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Cosmeceutical product ingredient labels (Patel Sun Exposure slide 50)</h5>
<p>The slide shows ingredient lists from cosmeceutical skincare products with several ingredients highlighted. She told the class to notice the highlighted ingredients and said they will see a lot of ascorbic acid, niacinamide, a retinoid and hyaluronic acid.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;you're gonna see a lot of ascorbic acid, niacinamide, retinoid, hyaluronic acid&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@74527</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Plant extracts in skincare (Patel Sun Exposure slide 51)</h5>
<p>The table pairs plant extracts with actions: green tea epicatechins (reduce UV erythema and cyclobutene pyrimidine dimers, antioxidant), soy isoflavones, grape seed polyphenols, coffee berry, ginkgo biloba (stimulates fibroblast and collagen production) and ginseng. She gave it as reference and said natural ingredients are marketed because patients do not want irritating ingredients; the effects she named are collagen, reduced melanin and UV protection.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="gobj" id="gobj-pl-u-14">
<h3>Dr. Patel, slide by slide · Sun exposure · slides 52&ndash;57</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds</span><b>The photoaging case answer, photoaging counseling, retinoid and hydroxy acid use, and her closing list</b><i>Patel Sun Exposure slide 52, 53, 54, 55, 56, 57</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Practice case: photoaging in a 65-year-old woman (Patel Sun Exposure slide 52)</h5>
<p>The case is a 65-year-old Fitzpatrick IV woman with wrinkles and brown spotting on the face, hands and forearms, a yellowish facial color, fine wrinkles deepening at the eyes and mouth, a smoking history (quit 15 years ago), a multivitamin, and calcium 600 mg with vitamin D 400 IU. The question asked which option improves hyperpigmentation. Her cue was the keyword hyperpigmentation, and the answer is hydroquinone, option C (T@76346 11/12), the depigmenting agent from slide 47.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;So keyword here, hyperpigmentation.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@76273</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Photoaging counseling points (Patel Sun Exposure slide 53)</h5>
<p>The slide says photoaging is preventable and cumulative, protect skin from further damage, use broad-spectrum SPF 15 or higher daily and reapply often, clean the face twice daily with a mild cleanser, use products in the order suggested, avoid eyes and mucous membranes, use moisturizers, adopt a healthy lifestyle and stop smoking, and expect results in at least four weeks. She summarized with two themes and pointed back to the sunscreen counseling points of slide 36 as applying here too.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Biggest things, prevention is key. Consistency is key.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@76403</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Photoaging counseling: hydroxy acid and retinoid products (Patel Sun Exposure slide 54)</h5>
<p>For hydroxy acid products apply to dry skin within 15 minutes of cleansing; adverse events are stinging, burning, itching, skin lightening and dryness. For retinoids, apply once at bedtime to dry skin after cleansing, add a moisturizer or foundation with SPF 30 or higher during the day, and expect scaling, redness, burning and dermatitis. She skipped through it for time and said to review it thoroughly, so the slide wording is the source. Her added point: the SPF is needed the day after a night retinoid.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Please do review the slide thoroughly.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@76557</span></p>
<p>&ldquo;don't skip out on that SPF the next day&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@76959</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Conclusions (Patel Sun Exposure slide 55)</h5>
<p>The slide says sunscreen ingredients are UV-length specific, products are labeled UVA, UVB, broad-spectrum or water resistant because of their ingredients, and the risks of sun exposure are real and preventable. In her wrap-up she listed what to know: the specific SPF amount, the patient's age, how to prevent exposure, and how a suspected skin cancer lesion looks, and told the class to remember the mnemonic, which in context is the ABCDE rule on slide 28.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Specific SPF amount, so pay attention to that, age of the patient&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/12 T@77144</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>
</details>
