<section class="gobj" id="gobj-ey-6">
<h3>Dr. Smith, slide by slide · Aqueous humor flow and the receptors that control it · slides 15&ndash;16</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds (Eye 15&ndash;16)</span><b>Aqueous humor leaves by two routes, and the ciliary body, trabecular meshwork and their receptors and enzyme are the targets of the glaucoma drugs</b><i>Eye slide 15, 16</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Aqueous humor flow: the two drainage routes (Eye slide 15)</h5>
<div class="g2-stats"><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">Labeled in the figure</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Trabecular meshwork, Schlemm&rsquo;s canal, the aqueous flow, ciliary body, iris, lens, sclera and chambers</span></div><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">The lecture sorts the outflow into</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Two routes</span></div></div><div class="g2-clsgrid"><div class="g2-cls g2-c2"><div class="g2-cls-h"><span class="g2-cls-t">Main route</span></div><div class="g2-stats"><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">Path</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Through the spongy trabecular meshwork into Schlemm&rsquo;s canal</span></div><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">Ends in</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Blood vessels</span></div></div></div><div class="g2-cls g2-c1"><div class="g2-cls-h"><span class="g2-cls-t">Minor route: uveoscleral</span></div><div class="g2-stats"><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">Path</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Fluid seeps between the uvea (the ciliary body, its ligaments and the iris) and the sclera</span></div><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">Ends</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Disperses through those tissues</span></div></div></div></div><div class="g2-stats"><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">The two routes</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Do not drain into the same place</span></div><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">Her spoken wording</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Varied; the slide labels are used</span></div></div>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;the most important outflow route is through that trabecular meshwork and Schlem's canal&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/13 T@32688</span></p>
<p>&ldquo;No, no, they don't drain into the same place.&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/13 T@32352</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Receptors and enzymes involved in aqueous humor (Eye slide 16)</h5>
<div class="g2-stats"><div class="g2-stat"><span class="g2-stat-k">Labeled in the figure</span><span class="g2-stat-v">Beta, alpha-2 and alpha-1 receptors, carbonic anhydrase, the ciliary blood supply, M3 and beta-2 receptors in the meshwork area, the uveoscleral outflow and the episcleral vein</span></div></div><p class="g2-p g2-capline">In lecture, where each target sits and what drugs do there:</p>
<ul>
Ciliary body epithelium (the cells that make aqueous humor): beta and alpha-2 receptors and carbonic anhydrase.</li><li>Blocking beta, activating alpha-2 or inhibiting carbonic anhydrase lowers production (inflow).
<li>Ciliary blood vessels: alpha-1 receptors.</li>
<li>Trabecular meshwork area: muscarinic M3 receptors, and possibly beta-2. Drugs here relax the stiff meshwork so more fluid flows out (outflow).</li>
</ul>
<p>How the receptors produce aqueous humor is not well known; the role of carbonic anhydrase is fairly well understood. All of them are drug targets.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;these receptors and the carbonic anhydrase enzyme are targets for the drugs&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/13 T@36962</span></p>
<p>&ldquo;make it less stiff, so that more of the aqueous humor can flow out&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/13 T@39537</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

