<section class="gobj" id="gobj-ey-23">
<h3>Dr. Smith, slide by slide · Ophthalmic antihistamine examples · slides 53&ndash;54</h3>
<div class="gbar"><span>What the lecture adds (Eye 53&ndash;54)</span><b>These two slides are the drug list for the class; she gave the details to tell the drugs apart, and said to pay attention to which ones have mast cell stabilization</b><i>Eye slide 53, 54</i></div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Alcaftadine, azelastine, bepotastine, cetirizine (Eye slide 53)</h5>
<ul>
<b>Alcaftadine.</b></li><li>The slide lists H4 antagonism.</li><li>Histamine at the H4 receptor on eosinophils draws them into the allergic response, so blocking H4 may add to its effect.</li><li>Little anticholinergic activity and little CNS penetration: few side effects.
<b>Azelastine.</b></li><li>An older antihistamine with a little anticholinergic activity that does reach the CNS.</li><li>She still uses it for itchy eyes and for allergic rhinitis, where it helps a stuffy nose.
<li><b>Bepotastine.</b> Both an H1 inverse agonist and a mast cell stabilizer, which is the one thing she asked to remember.</li>
<b>Cetirizine.</b></li><li>Minimal anticholinergic effect, so no dry mouth, but it can still cause drowsiness in sensitive people, which she called unusual for a second-generation antihistamine.</li><li>No mast cell stabilization, so it is the one not suggested for prophylactic use.
</ul>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;Pay special attention to which ones have mast cell stabilizing properties&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/17 T@48555</span></p>
<p>&ldquo;which one does not have mast cell stabilizing activity? Yep, cetirizine&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/17 T@51757</span></p></div>
</div>
<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5>Epinastine, ketotifen, olopatadine (Eye slide 54)</h5>
<ul>
<li><b>Epinastine.</b> The slide adds H2 antagonism for eyelid edema. Eyelid swelling is bothersome to patients, so epinastine is a topical choice for it.</li>
<li><b>Ketotifen.</b> A very good mast cell stabilizer.</li>
<li><b>Olopatadine.</b> Mast cell stabilization again, and, as the slide says, it also comes as a nasal spray for allergic rhinitis.</li>
</ul>
<p>Across the whole list, most of these drugs stabilize mast cells; cetirizine (Eye slide 53) is the exception.</p>
<div class='g2-said'><span class='g2-k'>She said</span>
<p>&ldquo;reducing eyelid swelling&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/17 T@51105</span></p>
<p>&ldquo;it's a real good mast cell stabilizer&rdquo; <span class='g2-src'>11/17 T@51460</span></p></div>
</div>
</section>

