<div class="gpt g2-block"><h5 id="lt4-3"><span class="g2-num">3.</span> What Dr. Lam added aloud (Lam Otic slide 16, 33, 34)</h5><details class="g2-fold"><summary><span class="g2-fold-s">Show this section</span></summary>
<div class="g2-said"><span class="g2-k">She said</span><p>On adults: &ldquo;If it&rsquo;s for adults. Lay down, rub it, drip it, drip it. Put a cotton ball, cotton over it just to keep it and let it stay for about a few minutes.&rdquo; <span class="g2-src">T@99534 11/20</span></p></div><div class="g2-said"><span class="g2-k">She said</span><p>On children: &ldquo;For children, you&rsquo;re willing to hold them down if possible and let them watch TV. Keep them still for a few minutes, because if they&rsquo;re running around, all of that solution is going to drip out.&rdquo; <span class="g2-src">T@99685 11/20</span></p></div><div class="g2-said"><span class="g2-k">She said</span><p>On when to refer: &ldquo;If they start having symptoms of an ear infection, which include ear discharge, or they have recent ear surgery, they need to go see their doctor or develop pain, burning sensation or irritation after medication use, they should see their doctor too.&rdquo; <span class="g2-src">T@100297 11/20</span></p></div><div class="g2-said"><span class="g2-k">She said</span><p>On the persistence limit: &ldquo;If the symptoms persist after 4 days, call your doctor.&rdquo; <span class="g2-src">T@96243 11/20</span></p></div>
</details></div>

